// Verifying MANY ECDSA P-256 signatures at once, in neant, on the curve arithmetic in
// src/neant/crypto/ec.nt and p256.nt. Not part of the boot image — load all five:
//   load "src/neant/crypto/bignum.nt"   load "src/neant/crypto/der.nt"
//   load "src/neant/crypto/ec.nt"       load "src/neant/crypto/p256.nt"
//   load "src/neant/crypto/batch.nt"
//
//   batchVerifyP256[keys;digests;sigs]     -> 1b / 0b      every signature valid?
//   batchVerifyP256Who[keys;digests;sigs]  -> a boolean per signature
//   batchVerifyP256Bad[keys;digests;sigs]  -> the indices that failed
//   batchVerifyP256Der[keys;digests;sigs]     the first two again, with each signature the DER
//   batchVerifyP256DerWho[keys;digests;sigs]  SEQUENCE { INTEGER r, INTEGER s } that a certificate
//                                             and a TLS CertificateVerify carry
//   batchVerifyP256Par[keys;digests;sigs;k] -> a boolean per signature, over k OS threads
//
// `keys` are SEC 1 uncompressed points, `digests` are 32-byte SHA-256 digests, and `sigs` are
// (r;s) pairs of big-endian magnitudes — exactly the arguments ecdsaVerifyP256 takes, one list
// each. Every entry point is total: a key that is not a point, an r or s outside [1,n-1], a digest
// of the wrong length or DER that does not parse is that signature answered 0b, never a signal and
// never a verdict on its neighbours. THE ANSWER IS PER SIGNATURE AND IT IS EXACT: these agree with
// ecdsaVerifyP256, signature by signature, on every input. ec.nt's single-signature path is
// untouched and is still what x509.nt and verify.nt call.
//
// ---- what a batch buys, measured on this machine, and what it does not
// (Cortex-X925, 20 cores; ./target/release/neant; the 512 real signatures in
// tests/data/p256-batch.txt, made by openssl, with the corpus repeated to fill the 1024- and
// 2048-lane rows. tests/bench.nt re-runs exactly this. Other agents were building on this machine
// while it ran, and a re-run lands within about 15% either way, so read these as a floor.)
//
//               one thread                          twenty threads, twenty independent batches
//   N      ms/signature   verify/s              ms/signature   verify/s   thread scaling
//      1      739.0           1.4                  80.25          12.5        8.9x
//      8       94.04         10.6                  10.99          91.0        8.6x
//     64       16.21         61.7                   1.622        616.6       10.0x
//    512        4.875       205.1                   0.449       2225.1       10.8x
//   1024        4.012       249.3                   0.363       2757.9       11.1x
//   2048        3.549       281.8                   0.314       3180.6       11.3x
//
// against, on the same machine and measured the same day:
//   ecdsaVerifyP256, one signature at a time     180.9 ms        5.5 verify/s    one core
//   openssl speed -seconds 3 ecdsap256            38.9 us    25684.0 verify/s    one core
//
// SO: THIS DOES NOT BEAT OPENSSL, and the terms matter more than the number. At its best — 2048
// signatures in one call, twenty OS threads, twenty cores — it verifies 3181 signatures a second
// against OpenSSL's 25684 on ONE core, which is 8x short; against OpenSSL on all twenty cores it
// is about 160x short. What it does do is close the gap from 4650x to 8x: one signature at a time
// this language is 180.9 ms against 38.9 us, and a batch across the machine is 0.314 ms of
// wall-clock per signature. That is 577x more signatures per second out of the same machine, and
// none of it is a new Rust primitive — bignum.nt, ec.nt and p256.nt are untouched.
// The batch is also SLOWER than ec.nt for a single signature: 739 ms against 180.9 ms, 4.1x worse,
// because the lane machinery below is all overhead when there is one lane to amortise it over. The
// crossover is at about four signatures. A caller with one signature should call ecdsaVerifyP256.
//
// ---- why this is not the batch verification the literature means, and why that is not a choice
// The batch verification of the textbooks — Naccache–M'Raïhi–Vaudenay–Raphaeli, Bellare–Garay–Rabin
// — checks N signatures with one multi-scalar multiplication: draw random coefficients a_i and test
//   SUM_i a_i*(u1_i*G + u2_i*Q_i - R_i)  =  O
// which is the point at infinity iff every term is, up to the chance that a forged set cancels,
// which is 2^-k for k-bit coefficients. That is a genuinely different algorithm and it is worth
// several-fold. IT IS NOT AVAILABLE FOR ECDSA, and not because of anything in this file.
//
// The sum above needs the POINT R_i. An ECDSA signature does not carry it: it carries
// r = x(R) mod n and nothing else. Lifting r back to a point costs a square root mod p — one
// exponentiation to (p+1)/4, about 384 modular multiplications, 8% of a verification, affordable —
// and then hands you the PAIR (x, ±y), because a square root has two. The sign is not recoverable
// from the signature, and not merely "not implemented here": (r,s) and (r,n-s) are BOTH valid
// signatures of the same message under the same key, because negating s negates u1 and u2 and so
// negates R, and x(-R) = x(R) = r. The two differ by exactly the sign that is wanted. So there is
// no function of (key, digest, r, s) that returns it, a batch of N has 2^N sign assignments, and a
// wrong guess makes a batch of honest signatures fail. Picking "even y" by convention would reject
// about half of all valid signatures. That is why every published ECDSA batch-verification
// algorithm — Cheon–Yi, Karati–Das — is stated for ECDSA* / ECDSA', the variant that transmits R
// instead of r; secp256k1's recovery byte is the same missing bit. Certificates and TLS carry
// plain ECDSA, so this file verifies plain ECDSA.
//
// Two consequences, both good, and both the opposite of what a randomised batch gives you:
//   * THERE IS NO SOUNDNESS ERROR. A randomised batch accepts a bad set with probability 2^-k and
//     needs its coefficients from `urand` rather than `rand` precisely because an attacker who can
//     predict them can build a set that cancels. Nothing below is randomised, so there is nothing
//     to predict and no 2^-k to argue about: each lane is decided by its own equation over its own
//     key and its own digest, and a lane that is wrong is wrong on its own.
//   * A FAILING BATCH SAYS WHICH ONE. A randomised batch returns one bit and a caller then has to
//     bisect or fall back to verifying individually; batchVerifyP256Bad returns the indices
//     outright, for the same price as the yes/no, because the per-lane answers were computed anyway.
// What was traded for that is the multi-scalar multiplication's saving, which is real: a Pippenger
// bucket sum over the 2N+1 points would cut the ~4900 modular multiplications a verification costs
// here to roughly 900. It is the single biggest thing left on the table, and it is behind one bit
// that the wire format does not have.
//
// ---- what it does instead: the array language, not the group theory
// The saving below is not in the number of modular multiplications — ec.nt pays about 4960 per
// verification (256 doublings at 8, ~192 mixed additions at 11, two Fermat inversions at ~800) and
// this pays about 4870 (256 doublings, 256 mixed additions because every lane adds at every step,
// and almost nothing for the inversion) — it is in what one of them costs. bnMontMul on 10 limbs of 26 bits is
// a dozen interpreted operations on ten-element vectors, and a ten-element vector operation is
// almost entirely dispatch: measured here, `c + a*b` costs 275 ns at length 10 and 7.2 us at length
// 5120, which is 27.5 ns per element against 1.41. So the batch is laid out LANE-MAJOR: a field
// element for the whole batch is `limbs` int vectors, one per 26-bit limb, each holding that limb
// of every signature. Every operation then runs the same schoolbook it always ran, on vectors N
// times longer, and the dispatch is amortised over N signatures instead of paid per signature:
//   bnMontMul, one signature at a time                        25.0 us
//   bfMul, per signature, at 16 / 256 / 512 / 1024 lanes   7.45 / 0.95 / 0.651 / 0.501 us
// 38x at 512 lanes, and that ratio is the whole of the table above. Nothing here is SIMD in the
// hardware sense and nothing was written in Rust; it is the same interpreter running the same
// primitives over longer vectors.
//
// Two things really are batch ALGORITHMS, and they are the ones an array language can hold:
//   * ONE MODULAR INVERSION FOR THE WHOLE BATCH (Montgomery's trick, bfInvAll). Each verification
//     needs s^-1 mod n, which ec.nt pays for with a Fermat exponentiation — about 415 modular
//     multiplications, and transliterated that would be 415 FULL-WIDTH batched multiplications, 9%
//     of the ladder, for something a batch need not pay per signature at all. Instead the lanes are
//     multiplied together up a binary tree (N/2 lanes, then N/4, ...), the one product at the root
//     is inverted once, and the inverse is pushed back down. About three full-width multiplications
//     plus one scalar inversion for the whole batch, and the tree is lane-halving, which is an
//     index operation here, not a copy.
//   * NO INVERSION MOD p AT ALL. ec.nt finishes by inverting Z to get the affine x, reducing it mod
//     n and comparing to r. This compares projectively instead: X = r*Z^2 mod p, or X = (r+n)*Z^2
//     when r+n is still below p. Those are exactly the two solutions of x mod n = r in [0,p),
//     because n < p < 2n on this curve, which is the case ec.nt's header warns is easy to get wrong
//     — it is handled here rather than skipped, and it costs three multiplications instead of an
//     inversion. tests/batch.nt asserts it on tests/data/p256-rx-ge-n.py's vectors.
//
// ---- the security argument
// Every check ec.nt makes, this makes, and a lane that fails one never reaches the arithmetic:
// bqLane refuses an r or s outside [1,n-1], a digest that is not 32 octets, and a public key that
// is not an on-curve uncompressed point with both coordinates below p — by calling ec.nt's own
// ecPoint, so there is one copy of that check in the tree. A refused lane is answered 0b and filled
// with a stand-in (r = s = e = 1, Q = G) so that the lanes around it still step together; the
// stand-in cannot change any other lane's answer, because no lane's arithmetic reads another lane.
// The ladder is Shamir's trick, ec.nt's, transliterated: 256 doublings and a mixed addition per
// step, over the same three-entry table (G, Q, G+Q). Two places need lane care and get it:
//   * dbl-2001-b needs no special case for the point at infinity — with Z = 0 it returns Z3 = 0 —
//     so bpDbl is uniform and ec.nt's `if` is an optimisation this file does not need.
//   * madd-2007-bl has two degenerate cases. A running total that is still the point at infinity is
//     selected past, lane by lane, and so is a step with nothing to add; the case it CANNOT compute
//     is equal points, where the formula divides by zero. bpMAdd returns a mask of the lanes that
//     hit it and bqVerify answers those with ecdsaVerify — ec.nt's own scalar verifier — rather
//     than with a number it computed wrongly. For honest signatures that mask is empty (it needs
//     the running total to land exactly on a table entry mid-ladder); for hostile ones the fallback
//     is what makes it not matter.
// Verification only, no signing, and NOTHING HERE IS CONSTANT-TIME — same as ec.nt, and for the
// same reason: every input a verifier sees is public. The lane layout makes that more visible, not
// less: the table lookup below is an index, so which entry a lane takes is a memory access pattern.
// There is no secret for it to leak.
//
// ---- the traps in the arithmetic, which are the same ones bignum.nt is written against
// A limb is 26 bits, so a product is < 2^52 and a column of `limbs` of them is < 2^55.4; the REDC
// loop adds at most another `limbs` products to a column, so a column stays under 2^56.4 and an
// exact i64 column has 2^62. bfMul masks T[i] to 26 bits before multiplying by np, which is the
// same m mod 2^26 the scalar code computes but without the intermediate that overflows. Every value
// below is fully reduced into [0,p) so that a limbwise comparison is a value comparison — bfIsZero
// and bfEq depend on it, and dropping the conditional subtraction after a multiplication would buy
// about 13% and cost that.

// ---- lane-major values -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A batched value is `limbs` int vectors: column j holds limb j of every lane, so lane l's number is
// {col[l]} each cols and an operation on all the lanes' limb j is one vector operation. Everything
// below carries its modulus in a context record with bignum.nt's names -- `p the modulus in limbs,
// `np its -N^-1 mod 2^26, `r2 R^2 mod N and `one R mod N -- so the same arithmetic runs mod p for
// the curve and mod n for the scalars. P256C is already such a record; bfCtx builds the one for n.
bfCtx: {[M]
  np: bnMontInv M[0]
  r2: bnMod[((2*count M)#0), 1; M]
  `p`np`r2`one!(M; np; r2; bnMontMul[,1; r2; M; np])}

bfLanes: {[A] count A[0]}
bfLift:  {[C;xs] nl: count C`p; + {bnPad[x;nl]} each xs}     // a list of limb lists -> the columns
bfLane:  {[A;i] bnTrim {x[i]} each A}                        // and one lane back out
bfCast:  {[C;v;n] nl: count C`p; {[w;k] n#w[k]}[bnPad[v;nl]] each til nl}   // one value in n lanes
bfPick:  {[A;idx] {[ix;col] col[ix]}[idx] each A}            // the lanes idx names, in that order
bfCat:   {[A;B] {[u;v] u,v}'[A;B]}                           // A's lanes then B's

// ---- lane-major modular arithmetic -------------------------------------------------------------
// Every value is fully reduced into [0,N): bfIsZero and bfEq compare limbs, and that is only a
// comparison of values when the representation is canonical. Leaving a multiplication's result
// unreduced (it is below 2N, and below 4N the next multiplication would still be exact) would save
// about 13% and cost exactly that.
//
// One carry pass, low column to high, each limb down to 26 bits. The columns may hold anything an
// i64 can: bfMul's are under 2^56.4. Unlike bnCarry this does not iterate -- one pass over columns
// that carry a scalar upward settles it, because the carry out of a column is at most 2^38 and the
// column above has 2^62 of room.
bfCarryCols: {[T]
  k: count T; c: 0; i: 0
  while[i<k; t: T[i]+c; T[i]: t band BNM; c: t shr BNB; i+:1]
  T,,c}

// One conditional subtraction of the modulus: R is nl carried columns plus a 0/1 top column, and
// the value is below 2N. The borrow chain runs to the end whatever the lanes hold -- t band BNM is
// the right limb for a negative t too, two's complement being what band reads -- and then the sign
// of the last borrow against the top column picks, per lane, between R and R-N.
bfCondSub: {[C;R;top]
  nl: count C`p; P: C`p
  D: R
  b: 0; j: 0
  while[j<nl; t: (R[j]-P[j])-b; D[j]: t band BNM; b: `int$ t<0; j+:1]
  s: `int$ not (top-b) < 0
  j: 0
  while[j<nl; R[j]: R[j] + s*D[j]-R[j]; j+:1]
  R}

bfAdd: {[C;A;B]
  nl: count C`p
  T: A; c: 0; j: 0
  while[j<nl; t: (A[j]+B[j])+c; T[j]: t band BNM; c: t shr BNB; j+:1]
  bfCondSub[C;T;c]}

// A-B: the borrow chain leaves (A-B) mod 2^(26*nl) in D and a borrow of 1 when A < B. Adding N back
// in exactly those lanes is right because the addition carries out past 2^(26*nl) again, and the
// carry out is what is dropped: (A-B+2^(26*nl)) + N is A-B+N below that modulus.
bfSub: {[C;A;B]
  nl: count C`p; P: C`p
  D: A; b: 0; j: 0
  while[j<nl; t: (A[j]-B[j])-b; D[j]: t band BNM; b: `int$ t<0; j+:1]
  c: 0; j: 0
  while[j<nl; t: (D[j] + b*P[j])+c; D[j]: t band BNM; c: t shr BNB; j+:1]
  D}

// A*B*R^-1 mod N, lane by lane: bnMontMul's schoolbook and REDC, one vector operation per pair of
// limbs instead of one per limb. A column takes nl products under 2^52 from the multiply and at most
// nl more from the reduction, so it stays under 2^56.4 and an exact i64 column has 2^62. The mask
// before np is what bnMontMul leaves to i64 wrap-around: only m mod 2^26 matters, and masking T[i]
// first keeps the product under 2^52 instead of over 2^81.
bfMul: {[C;A;B]
  nl: count C`p; P: C`p; np: C`np
  T: ((2*nl)+1)#,0*A[0]
  i: 0
  while[i<nl
    a: A[i]; j: 0
    while[j<nl; T[i+j]: T[i+j] + a*B[j]; j+:1]
    i+:1]
  i: 0
  while[i<nl
    m: BNM band np * BNM band T[i]
    j: 0
    while[j<nl; T[i+j]: T[i+j] + m*P[j]; j+:1]
    T[i+1]: T[i+1] + T[i] shr BNB
    i+:1]
  R: bfCarryCols (nl+1) # nl _ T
  bfCondSub[C; nl#R; R[nl]]}

bfSqr: {[C;A] bfMul[C;A;A]}

// 1 in the lanes where the value is zero, 0 elsewhere. Every value here is fully reduced, so a
// zero value is all-zero limbs and a limb comparison is a value comparison.
bfIsZero: {[A]
  s: A[0]; j: 1; nl: count A
  while[j<nl; s: s + A[j]; j+:1]
  `int$ s=0}
bfEq: {[A;B]
  e: `int$ A[0]=B[0]; j: 1; nl: count A
  while[j<nl; e: e * `int$ A[j]=B[j]; j+:1]
  e}
// per lane: s -> A, else B
bfSel: {[s;A;B] {[m;a;b] b + m*a-b}[s]'[A;B]}

// ---- one modular inversion for the whole batch -------------------------------------------------
// Montgomery's trick as a binary product tree. Lanes are multiplied pairwise up the tree -- N/2
// lanes, then N/4, down to one -- the single product at the root is inverted once with bignum.nt's
// Fermat exponentiation, and the inverse is pushed back down, each level's inverse times the other
// half giving this half's. About three full-width batched multiplications and one scalar inversion
// for the whole batch, against one 415-multiplication exponentiation per signature; and halving a
// lane vector is an index, not a copy. The lanes are padded up to a power of two with 1, which
// inverts to 1 and cannot disturb anything. EVERY LANE MUST BE NONZERO: bqPrep has already refused
// s = 0 and filled that lane with 1.
bfInvAll: {[C;A]
  n0: bfLanes A
  k: 1
  while[k<n0; k: 2*k]
  if[k>n0; A: bfCat[A; bfCast[C; C`one; k-n0]]]
  levels: ,A
  cur: A
  while[1 < bfLanes cur
    h: (bfLanes cur) div 2
    cur: bfMul[C; bfPick[cur; til h]; bfPick[cur; h+til h]]
    levels,: ,cur]
  tot: bnMontMul[bfLane[cur;0]; ,1; C`p; C`np]
  iv: bnMontMul[bnModExpL[tot; bnSub[C`p; ,2]; C`p]; C`r2; C`p; C`np]
  inv: bfLift[C; ,iv]
  i: (count levels) - 2
  while[not i<0
    cur: levels[i]
    h: (bfLanes cur) div 2
    inv: bfCat[bfMul[C; inv; bfPick[cur; h+til h]]; bfMul[C; inv; bfPick[cur; til h]]]
    i: i-1]
  bfPick[inv; til n0]}

// ---- the group, one lane per signature ---------------------------------------------------------
bp3Sel: {[s;A;B] {[m;a;b] bfSel[m;a;b]}[s]'[A;B]}

bpDbl: {[C;R]
  d: bfSqr[C;R[2]]
  g: bfSqr[C;R[1]]
  bt: bfMul[C;R[0];g]
  al: bfMul[C; bfSub[C;R[0];d]; bfAdd[C;R[0];d]]
  al: bfAdd[C; al; bfAdd[C;al;al]]
  b4: bfAdd[C;bt;bt]; b4: bfAdd[C;b4;b4]
  x3: bfSub[C; bfSqr[C;al]; bfAdd[C;b4;b4]]
  z3: bfSub[C; bfSub[C; bfSqr[C; bfAdd[C;R[1];R[2]]]; g]; d]
  g8: bfSqr[C;g]; g8: bfAdd[C;g8;g8]; g8: bfAdd[C;g8;g8]; g8: bfAdd[C;g8;g8]
  y3: bfSub[C; bfMul[C; al; bfSub[C;b4;x3]]; g8]
  (x3;y3;z3)}

// Jacobian R plus affine (Ax;Ay), in the lanes where dd is 1. Returns the point and the mask of
// lanes that hit the equal-points case madd-2007-bl cannot compute: see the header.
bpMAdd: {[C;R;Ax;Ay;dd]
  nn: bfLanes Ax
  one: bfCast[C; C`one; nn]
  zer: bfCast[C; BNZERO; nn]
  zz: bfSqr[C;R[2]]
  u2: bfMul[C;Ax;zz]
  s2: bfMul[C;Ay; bfMul[C;R[2];zz]]
  h:  bfSub[C;u2;R[0]]
  rr: bfSub[C;s2;R[1]]
  zinf: bfIsZero R[2]
  hz: bfIsZero h
  rz: bfIsZero rr
  r2v: bfAdd[C;rr;rr]
  hh: bfSqr[C;h]
  ii: bfAdd[C;hh;hh]; ii: bfAdd[C;ii;ii]
  jj: bfMul[C;h;ii]
  vv: bfMul[C;R[0];ii]
  x3: bfSub[C; bfSub[C; bfSqr[C;r2v]; jj]; bfAdd[C;vv;vv]]
  tt: bfMul[C;R[1];jj]
  y3: bfSub[C; bfMul[C;r2v; bfSub[C;vv;x3]]; bfAdd[C;tt;tt]]
  z3: bfSub[C; bfSub[C; bfSqr[C; bfAdd[C;R[2];h]]; zz]; hh]
  O: (x3;y3;z3)
  O: bp3Sel[hz; (one;one;zer); O]
  O: bp3Sel[zinf; (Ax;Ay;one); O]
  O: bp3Sel[dd; O; R]
  (O; dd * hz * rz * 1-zinf)}

// ---- Shamir's trick, every lane stepping together ----------------------------------------------
// The three-entry table is laid out flat, entry-fastest, so the table lookup for a whole batch is
// one index per limb instead of a per-lane branch: lane l's entry j sits at 3*l+j.
bpFlat: {[U;V;W]
  nn: count U[0]
  i0: 3*til nn
  nl: count U
  F: nl#,0
  k: 0
  while[k<nl
    f: (3*nn)#0
    f[i0]: U[k]; f[i0+1]: V[k]; f[i0+2]: W[k]
    F[k]: f
    k+:1]
  F}
bpGather: {[F;idx] {[ix;col] col[ix]}[idx] each F}

// b1 and b2 are `bits` lane vectors of 0/1, most significant first. t3inf marks the lanes whose
// G+Q is the point at infinity, which is an addition to skip rather than an addition to make.
bpShamir: {[C;b1;b2;T1;T2;T3;t3inf]
  nn: bfLanes T1[0]
  FX: bpFlat[T1[0];T2[0];T3[0]]
  FY: bpFlat[T1[1];T2[1];T3[1]]
  b0: 3*til nn
  R: (bfCast[C; C`one; nn]; bfCast[C; C`one; nn]; bfCast[C; BNZERO; nn])
  deg: nn#0
  i: 0; nb: count b1
  while[i<nb
    R: bpDbl[C;R]
    jj: b1[i] + 2*b2[i]
    jp: `int$ jj>0
    dd: jp * 1 - t3inf * `int$ jj=3
    idx: b0 + jj - jp
    md: bpMAdd[C; R; bpGather[FX;idx]; bpGather[FY;idx]; dd]
    R: md[0]
    deg: deg | md[1]
    i+:1]
  (R;deg)}

// ---- the batch verifier ------------------------------------------------------------------------
// bit i of every lane at once, least significant first, off lane-major limbs
bfBit: {[U;i] 1 band U[i div BNB] shr i mod BNB}

// The per-signature checks that are decisions rather than arithmetic: r and s in [1,n-1], the
// digest the right length, the public key an on-curve uncompressed point with both coordinates
// below p -- ec.nt's own ecPoint, so there is one copy of that check in the tree. () is no, for
// every reason, exactly as ec.nt's ecVerify answers 0b for every reason. One list argument because
// `@` below applies it to one, and `@` is here for the same belt-and-braces ec.nt wraps its own
// exports in: bignum.nt signals on an operand wider than it can multiply exactly, and a signature
// does not get to turn that into an exception the caller has to catch.
bqLane: {[a]
  C: a[0]; k: a[1]; dg: a[2]; sg: a[3]
  if[not (C`dgst) = count dg; :()]
  rv: bnFromBytes sg[0]
  sd: bnFromBytes sg[1]
  if[bnIsZero rv; :()]
  if[bnIsZero sd; :()]
  if[not 0 > bnCmp[rv; C`n]; :()]
  if[not 0 > bnCmp[sd; C`n]; :()]
  Q: ecPoint[C; k]
  if[0 = count Q; :()]
  (rv; sd; bnMod[bnFromBytes dg; C`n]; Q[0]; Q[1])}

// A lane that bqLane refused is answered 0b without the batch ever seeing it, and is filled with a
// well-formed stand-in -- r = s = e = 1 and Q = G -- so that the lanes around it still step
// together. The stand-in cannot change any other lane's answer: no lane's arithmetic reads another
// lane, and its own answer was already decided here.
bqPrep: {[C;keys;digests;sigs]
  nn: count sigs
  ok: nn#0; rl: (); sl: (); el: (); qx: (); qy: ()
  i: 0
  while[i<nn
    v: @[bqLane; (C; keys[i]; digests[i]; sigs[i]); {()}]
    good: 0 < count v
    ok[i]: `int$ good
    if[not good; v: (,1; ,1; ,1; C`gx; C`gy)]
    rl,: ,(v[0])
    sl,: ,(v[1])
    el,: ,(v[2])
    qx,: ,(v[3])
    qy,: ,(v[4])
    i+:1]
  `ok`r`s`e`qx`qy!(ok;rl;sl;el;qx;qy)}

// r as a field element, and r+n when that is still below p: x mod n = r has exactly these two
// solutions in [0,p), because n < p < 2n on both NIST curves here. Checking X against r*Z^2 and
// (r+n)*Z^2 is what lets the batch finish with no inversion mod p at all.
bqTargets: {[C;rs]
  a: {[C;v] efTo[C; v]}[C] each rs
  hi: {[C;v] w: bnAdd[v; C`n]; $[0 > bnCmp[w; C`p]; efTo[C;w]; BNZERO]}[C] each rs
  m: `int$ {[C;v] 0 > bnCmp[bnAdd[v; C`n]; C`p]}[C] each rs
  (a; hi; m)}

// G+Q per lane, affine, plus the mask of lanes where it is the point at infinity (Q = -G). The one
// inversion this needs is the batch one. G+G is the other degenerate case: 2G is a constant.
bqTable: {[C;T1;T2]
  nn: bfLanes T1[0]
  one: bfCast[C; C`one; nn]
  md: bpMAdd[C; (T1[0];T1[1];one); T2[0]; T2[1]; nn#1]
  J: md[0]
  zinf: bfIsZero J[2]
  Zs: bfSel[zinf; one; J[2]]
  zi: bfInvAll[C; Zs]
  z2: bfSqr[C;zi]
  ax: bfMul[C; J[0]; z2]
  ay: bfMul[C; J[1]; bfMul[C;z2;zi]]
  dg: ecToAffine[C; ecDbl[C; (C`gx; C`gy; C`one)]]
  ax: bfSel[md[1]; bfCast[C; dg[0]; nn]; ax]
  ay: bfSel[md[1]; bfCast[C; dg[1]; nn]; ay]
  ((ax;ay); zinf * 1 - md[1])}

// The whole batch, as one answer per signature. The order is: the per-signature refusals, then the
// scalars (one inversion mod n for the batch, then u1 = e*w and u2 = r*w and their bits), then the
// table, then the ladder, then the projective comparison -- and finally the handful of lanes, if
// any, that the mixed addition could not compute, answered by ec.nt's scalar verifier.
bqVerify: {[C;keys;digests;sigs]
  nn: count sigs
  if[nn=0; :()]
  pp: bqPrep[C;keys;digests;sigs]
  Cn: bfCtx C`n
  r2n: bfCast[Cn; Cn`r2; nn]
  onen: bfCast[Cn; ,1; nn]
  Sm: bfMul[Cn; bfLift[Cn; pp`s]; r2n]
  W: bfInvAll[Cn; Sm]
  U1: bfMul[Cn; bfMul[Cn; bfMul[Cn; bfLift[Cn; pp`e]; r2n]; W]; onen]
  U2: bfMul[Cn; bfMul[Cn; bfMul[Cn; bfLift[Cn; pp`r]; r2n]; W]; onen]
  nb: C`bits
  b1: {[U;i] bfBit[U;i]}[U1] each reverse til nb
  b2: {[U;i] bfBit[U;i]}[U2] each reverse til nb
  T1: (bfCast[C; C`gx; nn]; bfCast[C; C`gy; nn])
  T2: (bfLift[C; pp`qx]; bfLift[C; pp`qy])
  t3: bqTable[C; T1; T2]
  sh: bpShamir[C; b1; b2; T1; T2; t3[0]; t3[1]]
  R: sh[0]
  tg: bqTargets[C; pp`r]
  Z2: bfSqr[C; R[2]]
  c1: bfEq[R[0]; bfMul[C; bfLift[C; tg[0]]; Z2]]
  c2: (tg[2]) * bfEq[R[0]; bfMul[C; bfLift[C; tg[1]]; Z2]]
  res: (pp`ok) * (1 - bfIsZero R[2]) * c1 | c2
  // a lane that hit the equal-points case is answered by ec.nt's own verifier instead
  bad: where 0 < sh[1]
  i: 0
  while[i < count bad
    k: bad[i]
    res[k]: `int$ ecdsaVerify[C; keys[k]; digests[k]; sigs[k][0]; sigs[k][1]]
    i+:1]
  res}

// ---- the exports -------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A DER signature that does not parse is a lane answered 0b, exactly as ecdsaVerifyP256Der answers
// it: the zero r below fails bqPrep's range check and nothing about it reaches the arithmetic.
bqDerSplit: {[sigs] {[d] rs: @[ecSigRS; d; {()}]; $[2 = count rs; rs; (0x00;0x00)]} each sigs}

batchVerifyP256:    {[keys;digests;sigs] all 0 < bqVerify[P256C;keys;digests;sigs]}
batchVerifyP256Who: {[keys;digests;sigs] 0 < bqVerify[P256C;keys;digests;sigs]}
batchVerifyP256Bad: {[keys;digests;sigs] where 0 = bqVerify[P256C;keys;digests;sigs]}
batchVerifyP256Der:    {[keys;digests;sigs] batchVerifyP256[keys;digests; bqDerSplit sigs]}
batchVerifyP256DerWho: {[keys;digests;sigs] batchVerifyP256Who[keys;digests; bqDerSplit sigs]}

// ---- across cores ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// One OS thread per chunk, each with its own batch. `Value` is Arc-refcounted and copy-on-write, so
// the lanes cross the thread boundary with no copy and no lock, and the chunks are disjoint, so
// there is nothing for `shared` to be needed on.
//
// THE ONE THING THAT IS SHARED, AND IT COSTS 2.7x. A spawned thread forks a snapshot of the
// caller's globals, and a global holding a function holds an `Arc<FnCode>` — the same FnCode object
// in every thread. FnCode carries the tracing JIT's per-loop-header state behind a `Mutex`
// (src/value.rs, `loop_action`), and the interpreter takes that mutex on EVERY backward jump. The
// loops below run hundreds of millions of backward jumps, so twenty threads running the same hot
// loop queue on one lock. Measured on this machine, a batch of 64 across 20 threads:
//   the forked snapshot, one shared FnCode      224 verify/s    3.6x over one thread
//   each thread re-`load`s this file first      611 verify/s   10.9x over one thread
// so the spawned body re-loads the module, which gives that thread its own FnCode objects and
// nothing to queue on. BATCHNT is where it is loaded from; a caller running from somewhere other
// than the repository root sets it. A load that fails is not fatal — the batch is still correct,
// it is only back to sharing the lock — which is why it goes through `@`.
// The fix that would make this unnecessary is in the VM rather than here: the two states that
// matter on a hot loop, `Compiled` and `Rejected`, are write-once, so they could be read without
// taking the mutex at all. That file belongs to the JIT work, not to this one.
BATCHNT: "src/neant/crypto/batch.nt"

// Threads cost lanes, too: a batch split k ways has k times fewer lanes per vector operation, and
// the dispatch is amortised over those lanes, so splitting ONE batch k ways is worth far less than
// k. Measured on the 512-signature corpus: 2512 ms in one thread, 1818 ms split four ways (1.38x),
// 1967 ms split twenty (1.29x) -- four threads beat twenty. This entry point is for a caller who has
// one batch and wants it sooner; twenty INDEPENDENT batches is the shape that scales, and that is
// the twenty-thread column in the header's table.
batchVerifyP256Par: {[keys;digests;sigs;nthr]
  nn: count sigs
  if[nn=0; :0#1b]
  per: 1 | ceiling (0.0+nn) % 1 | nthr
  parts: `int$ per cut til nn
  hs: {[keys;digests;sigs;ix]
    spawn {@[load; BATCHNT; {0}]; batchVerifyP256Who[keys[ix]; digests[ix]; sigs[ix]]}}[keys;digests;sigs] each parts
  raze {join x} each hs}
